    also_known_as:  ['Special Measures', 'Special measures regime', 'Inadequate', 'Recovery', 'Improvement required', 'CQC monitoring'],
    description: `Special Measures is a CQC regime applied to services rated Inadequate Overall or where there are serious concerns about safety. When a service enters Special Measures, CQC provides intensified monitoring and the provider is given a defined period (typically 6 months for adult social care) to make significant improvements before CQC considers cancelling the service's registration. Special Measures is the last step before closure.`,
    inspector_focus: `When a service is in Special Measures, CQC will conduct more frequent monitoring — which may include revisits, desk-based reviews, and review of notifications and data. The provider must demonstrate rapid and substantial improvement across the areas of concern. CQC will typically publish an updated report following a monitoring visit. If improvement is insufficient within the defined timeframe, CQC will move toward cancellation of registration.`,
    evidence_expected: `If a provider is in Special Measures, they should produce a detailed improvement plan with specific actions, owners, and timescales for each finding. This plan is submitted to CQC and should be monitored by the provider's governance structure. Evidence of improvement against the plan should be available at the monitoring visit.`,
    rating_indicators: `A service can exit Special Measures if a subsequent inspection rates it as Good or better Overall, or if CQC is satisfied that sufficient improvement has been made across the Inadequate areas. Exiting Special Measures requires genuine, evidenced improvement across all five key questions, not just the specific areas initially rated Inadequate.`,
    source_urls: ['https://www.cqc.org.uk/guidance-providers/residential-adult-social-care/special-measures-adult-social-care', 'https://www.cqc.org.uk/guidance-providers/regulations-enforcement/our-enforcement-approach'],
